[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/dd2581c4-bcb8-4930-8eee-ad65370320a5/studies/e245b9e3-d93c-4851-8d70-02428862a893/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Stonewall Dutch! In this chapter, we'll take a look at the Stonewall setup for Black in the Dutch when White develops both knights to c3 and f3. It is a very solid setup that is easy to play, usually consisting of pawns on the light squares such as f5, e6, d5 and c6. Let's get started!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White places two pawns in the center and grabs more space with c4. We'll develop our knight and take more control of the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {You'll come across Nc3 71% of the time, it being the most popular move. White develops the knight as well and contests some of the central squares. As per usual, we'll focus on developing the kingside first. Here, we strengthen the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {This is the most common move in this position, played about 30% of the time. We place a pawn in the center of the board! Now we've got full control of the e4-square, and are planning to solidify the center with ...c6. However, this weakens the e5-square, and White often takes advantage of that and places a piece there!} 4...d5 5.Bf4 {The most popular move by White, played 66% of the time, is Bf4. Placing a lot of pawns on light squares means we've weakened the dark squares, so White takes control of those immediately! I recommend ...c6 here, providing more protection to the center.} 5...c6 6.e3 {White will usually continue in a natural manner, opening up the light-squared bishop... And one of the key moves here is not to get passive with ...Be7, but rather challenge White's active bishop with ...Bd6! We don't mind a trade here, although White has multiple options.} 6...Bd6 7.Bxd6 {If they capture, at least we've gotten rid of their active bishop. We'll recapture with the queen. One of the main ideas for the near future would be to achieve the ...e5-push and grab even more space in the center, after completely controlling the e4-square.} 7...Qxd6 8.Bd3 {Another move you could face is Bd3, activating the bishop and putting pressure on the f5-pawn. This doesn't allow us to play ...e5 immediately, as our f5-pawn would be loose, although defended. That's why I recommend defending the f5-pawn here with ...g6. Although it weakens the kingside, the prospects of ...Nbd7 and ...e5 are extremely powerful.} 8...g6 9.O-O {White would eventually castle. We'd continue development and control the e5-square with one more piece. Now pushing ...e5 is definitely on the cards, unless White captures in the center, as you'll see.} 9...Nbd7 10.Rc1 {White might prepare that with Rc1, taking control of the c-file. We'll simply bring the king to safety.} 10...O-O 11.cxd5 {Now it makes sense for White to capture in the center. Capturing with the c-pawn would let White get a very active rook on c1, and access to the b5-square, so I recommend capturing with the e-pawn. Now we won't be able to achieve the ...e5-push, but at least we've opened up the e-file and can shove the knight into the e4-square.} 11...exd5 12.a3 {Not having made us recapture in the center with the c-pawn, one of the main plans for White is to expand on the queenside. They are aiming for b4. We'll get an extremely strong knight on the e4-square, though. White can't capture with the knight as they would get forked!} 12...Ne4 13.b4 {They could continue with b4, though. Once they do...} 13...a5 {Remember to challenge the b4-pawn right away! Our position is comfortable, with the knight coming to f6 to support the knight on e4. The only problem is the bishop on c8. On the other hand, White might play Qb3 to develop and instigate on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/dd2581c4-bcb8-4930-8eee-ad65370320a5/studies/e245b9e3-d93c-4851-8d70-02428862a893/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Stonewall Dutch! In this chapter, we'll take a look at the Stonewall setup for Black in the Dutch when White develops both knights to c3 and f3. It is a very solid setup that is easy to play, usually consisting of pawns on the light squares such as f5, e6, d5 and c6. Let's get started!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White places two pawns in the center and grabs more space with c4. We'll develop our knight and take more control of the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {You'll come across Nc3 71% of the time, it being the most popular move. White develops the knight as well and contests some of the central squares. As per usual, we'll focus on developing the kingside first. Here, we strengthen the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {This is the most common move in this position, played about 30% of the time. We place a pawn in the center of the board! Now we've got full control of the e4-square, and are planning to solidify the center with ...c6. However, this weakens the e5-square, and White often takes advantage of that and places a piece there!} 4...d5 5.Bf4 {The most popular move by White, played 66% of the time, is Bf4. Placing a lot of pawns on light squares means we've weakened the dark squares, so White takes control of those immediately! I recommend ...c6 here, providing more protection to the center.} 5...c6 6.e3 {White will usually continue in a natural manner, opening up the light-squared bishop... And one of the key moves here is not to get passive with ...Be7, but rather challenge White's active bishop with ...Bd6! We don't mind a trade here, although White has multiple options.} 6...Bd6 7.Bxd6 {If they capture, at least we've gotten rid of their active bishop. We'll recapture with the queen. One of the main ideas for the near future would be to achieve the ...e5-push and grab even more space in the center, after completely controlling the e4-square.} 7...Qxd6 8.c5 {One of the approaches for White is to try and close down the center with tempo on our queen. They want to play positionally and with space. Retreating the queen to e7 is good, still keeping an eye on the e5-square. Knowing we want to play ...e5, White might play Ne5 themselves first!} 8...Qe7 9.Bd3 {A normal developing move by White, preparing castling. White attacks the f5-pawn and prevents ...e5 for the moment, if we develop our queenside knight. I recommend developing the knight here nevertheless and controlling that crucial e5-square.} 9...Nbd7 10.O-O {If White castles... Let's go for the ...g6-idea, enabling ...e5 again! This is an important concept I want you to remember.} 10...g6 11.b4 {White can't stop ...e5 now, but they can continue expanding on the queenside. Let's create chaos in the center! We are threatening ...e4 here...} 11...e5 12.dxe5 {So it's probably best for White to capture in the center. Naturally, we'll recapture with the knight.} 12...Nxe5 13.Nxe5 {White will often trade here... But that lets us centralize our queen with an attack on the knight on c3! The queen in the center now also targets the h2-pawn, and we could use that to create a small attack.} 13...Qxe5 14.Qc2 {One of the lines is for White to activate the queen and defend the knight. Let's attack the h2-pawn immediately!} 14...Ng4 15.g3 {This forces White to play g3, weakening their light squares on the kingside. And we've still got our light-squared bishop!} 15...O-O {Castling here gives us a comfortable position. We've got a lot of space in the center and active pieces. Next, we'll activate the light-squared bishop and connect the rooks.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/dd2581c4-bcb8-4930-8eee-ad65370320a5/studies/e245b9e3-d93c-4851-8d70-02428862a893/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Stonewall Dutch! In this chapter, we'll take a look at the Stonewall setup for Black in the Dutch when White develops both knights to c3 and f3. It is a very solid setup that is easy to play, usually consisting of pawns on the light squares such as f5, e6, d5 and c6. Let's get started!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White places two pawns in the center and grabs more space with c4. We'll develop our knight and take more control of the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {You'll come across Nc3 71% of the time, it being the most popular move. White develops the knight as well and contests some of the central squares. As per usual, we'll focus on developing the kingside first. Here, we strengthen the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {This is the most common move in this position, played about 30% of the time. We place a pawn in the center of the board! Now we've got full control of the e4-square, and are planning to solidify the center with ...c6. However, this weakens the e5-square, and White often takes advantage of that and places a piece there!} 4...d5 5.Bf4 {The most popular move by White, played 66% of the time, is Bf4. Placing a lot of pawns on light squares means we've weakened the dark squares, so White takes control of those immediately! I recommend ...c6 here, providing more protection to the center.} 5...c6 6.e3 {White will usually continue in a natural manner, opening up the light-squared bishop... And one of the key moves here is not to get passive with ...Be7, but rather challenge White's active bishop with ...Bd6! We don't mind a trade here, although White has multiple options.} 6...Bd6 7.Bxd6 {If they capture, at least we've gotten rid of their active bishop. We'll recapture with the queen. One of the main ideas for the near future would be to achieve the ...e5-push and grab even more space in the center, after completely controlling the e4-square.} 7...Qxd6 8.c5 {One of the approaches for White is to try and close down the center with tempo on our queen. They want to play positionally and with space. Retreating the queen to e7 is good, still keeping an eye on the e5-square. Knowing we want to play ...e5, White might play Ne5 themselves first!} 8...Qe7 9.Bd3 {A normal developing move by White, preparing castling. White attacks the f5-pawn and prevents ...e5 for the moment, if we develop our queenside knight. I recommend developing the knight here nevertheless and controlling that crucial e5-square.} 9...Nbd7 10.Ne5 {White could choose to play more aggressively and play Ne5 instead of castling here. We won't let that knight stay on the active e5-square for long! That's why we played ...Nd7, after all.} 10...Nxe5 11.dxe5 {White will recapture, naturally...} 11...Ng4 {And I recommend ...Ng4 here, with an attack on the e5-pawn. White's c5-pawn is also hanging! How are they going to defend their weaknesses? Something like f4 hangs the e3-pawn as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/dd2581c4-bcb8-4930-8eee-ad65370320a5/studies/e245b9e3-d93c-4851-8d70-02428862a893/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Stonewall Dutch! In this chapter, we'll take a look at the Stonewall setup for Black in the Dutch when White develops both knights to c3 and f3. It is a very solid setup that is easy to play, usually consisting of pawns on the light squares such as f5, e6, d5 and c6. Let's get started!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White places two pawns in the center and grabs more space with c4. We'll develop our knight and take more control of the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {You'll come across Nc3 71% of the time, it being the most popular move. White develops the knight as well and contests some of the central squares. As per usual, we'll focus on developing the kingside first. Here, we strengthen the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {This is the most common move in this position, played about 30% of the time. We place a pawn in the center of the board! Now we've got full control of the e4-square, and are planning to solidify the center with ...c6. However, this weakens the e5-square, and White often takes advantage of that and places a piece there!} 4...d5 5.Bg5 {5.Bg5 is played about 12% of the time, but people love to pin the knight on f6, especially when it threatens to come to e4. I simply recommend ...c6 here, strengthening the center and completing our pawn setup in the Stonewall Dutch.} 5...c6 6.e3 {White's usual continuation is e3, opening the light-squared bishop. It most often develops to the d3-square. Activating the dark-squared bishop and unpinning the knight.} 6...Be7 7.Bd3 {White continues developing too, ready to castle. This line is very positional, with our key idea being to place the knight on the e4-square we control. Let's castle first.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White brings the king to safety as well. Now the knight is in the center of the board!} 8...Ne4 9.Bxe4 {If White chooses to remove the knight immediately... We'll recapture with the f-pawn. This way a file for our rook opens up and we attack the knight on f3. There is still the tension between the dark-squared bishops.} 9...fxe4 10.Bxe7 {White basically has to capture on e7 now and attack our queen. We'll take back, naturally.} 10...Qxe7 11.Ne5 {If they place the knight in the center, you should know how to respond by now. Activate your own knight and challenge White's! White can't defend the knight with f4 here because we could capture en passant.} 11...Nd7 12.Nxd7 {So they would probably capture on d7.} 12...Bxd7 {And let's recapture. Our course of action should be to make use of the open f-file and rotate our rooks to the h6- and f6-squares. That way, we can attack the king!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/dd2581c4-bcb8-4930-8eee-ad65370320a5/studies/e245b9e3-d93c-4851-8d70-02428862a893/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Stonewall Dutch! In this chapter, we'll take a look at the Stonewall setup for Black in the Dutch when White develops both knights to c3 and f3. It is a very solid setup that is easy to play, usually consisting of pawns on the light squares such as f5, e6, d5 and c6. Let's get started!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White places two pawns in the center and grabs more space with c4. We'll develop our knight and take more control of the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {You'll come across Nc3 71% of the time, it being the most popular move. White develops the knight as well and contests some of the central squares. As per usual, we'll focus on developing the kingside first. Here, we strengthen the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {This is the most common move in this position, played about 30% of the time. We place a pawn in the center of the board! Now we've got full control of the e4-square, and are planning to solidify the center with ...c6. However, this weakens the e5-square, and White often takes advantage of that and places a piece there!} 4...d5 5.Bf4 {The most popular move by White, played 66% of the time, is Bf4. Placing a lot of pawns on light squares means we've weakened the dark squares, so White takes control of those immediately! I recommend ...c6 here, providing more protection to the center.} 5...c6 6.e3 {White will usually continue in a natural manner, opening up the light-squared bishop... And one of the key moves here is not to get passive with ...Be7, but rather challenge White's active bishop with ...Bd6! We don't mind a trade here, although White has multiple options.} 6...Bd6 7.Ne5 {White could choose to keep the dark-squared bishops on the board and play Ne5! Capturing the knight with ...Bxe5 would only give White the active bishop on e5. We can simply castle instead. Although White has some space, they don't have any immediate threats.} 7...O-O 8.Bd3 {They continue developing with Bd3, ready to castle. We are a bit cramped, but we can still develop! Next, we'll try to get the knight into the game and possibly trade on e5.} 8...Qe7 9.O-O {White castles... And we'll do just that, challenging the knight on e5. If White lets us capture, they'll destabilize their own center.} 9...Nbd7 10.c5 {If White tries c5 now, it's different, as our knight is already on d7. In this instance, we can capture on e5. If White captures with the bishop, we'll trade it for our knight on d7.} 10...Bxe5 11.dxe5 {That's why they'll probably recapture with the pawn. But now their bishop on f4 is blocked in and basically useless.} 11...Ng4 {Also, capturing with the d-pawn weakens the c5-pawn as well. Now, we attack both the e5- and the c5-pawns! White can't defend both.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The 2 Knights Setup / Study 1: White Plays 5.Bg5 or 5.Bf4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/dd2581c4-bcb8-4930-8eee-ad65370320a5/studies/e245b9e3-d93c-4851-8d70-02428862a893/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Stonewall Dutch! In this chapter, we'll take a look at the Stonewall setup for Black in the Dutch when White develops both knights to c3 and f3. It is a very solid setup that is easy to play, usually consisting of pawns on the light squares such as f5, e6, d5 and c6. Let's get started!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White places two pawns in the center and grabs more space with c4. We'll develop our knight and take more control of the e4-square.} 2...Nf6 3.Nc3 {You'll come across Nc3 71% of the time, it being the most popular move. White develops the knight as well and contests some of the central squares. As per usual, we'll focus on developing the kingside first. Here, we strengthen the center and open up the dark-squared bishop.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {This is the most common move in this position, played about 30% of the time. We place a pawn in the center of the board! Now we've got full control of the e4-square, and are planning to solidify the center with ...c6. However, this weakens the e5-square, and White often takes advantage of that and places a piece there!} 4...d5 5.Bg5 {5.Bg5 is played about 12% of the time, but people love to pin the knight on f6, especially when it threatens to come to e4. I simply recommend ...c6 here, strengthening the center and completing our pawn setup in the Stonewall Dutch.} 5...c6 6.cxd5 {Instead of developing with e3, White could choose to resolve matters in the center immediately with cxd5! Capturing with the e-pawn maintains a nice pawn chain and keeps control of the b5-square.} 6...exd5 7.e3 {Now White can play e3, and we're back to the usual ideas. Unpinning the knight and preparing ...Ne4 in the future. We are ready to castle.} 7...Be7 8.Bd3 {White activates their bishop to d3, with an attack on the f5-pawn. Once we castle...} 8...O-O 9.O-O {White could do the same. Now that most of the pieces have been developed, what's the key move we want to play in the center? Getting the knight to the great outpost.} 9...Ne4 10.Bxe7 {White captures... And we'll capture back.} 10...Qxe7 11.Ne5 {If they shove the knight into the center, our main idea is ...Nd7. In this instance, we can activate the light-squared bishop first.} 11...Be6 12.f4 {Solidifying the knight on e5. Now let's activate our own knight and challenge the knight on e5.} 12...Nd7 13.Bxe4 {Should White choose to get rid of our dangerous knight in the center...} 13...dxe4 {Now our light-squared bishop comes to life, which is always a good achievement in the Stonewall Dutch! Our plan now is simple: get the rooks to the d- and the c-files, and play for ...c5! Now that we captured ...dxe4, ...c5 is the key pawn break we want to achieve, breaking apart White's already shaky structure in the center.} *

