1.d4 {In this chapter, we'll take a look at how to play our already known Stonewall Dutch if White decides to go for various Nf3 setups. Let's dive right in!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White takes advantage of being first to move and places two pawns in the center... ...And we'll build behind the f-pawn to activate our first piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {Nf3 is the third most popular move, being played 17% of the time, but the positions often transpose into the 2 Knights setups. However, if White knows where they're going with this, it's crucial for you to know how to play against the Nf3 setups! We create a pawn chain and open up the dark-squared bishop. We'll have a pawn coming to d5 next!} 3...e6 4.g3 {In this study, we'll cover 4.g3 by White, being the most popular move played 62% of the time. White wants to fianchetto in Catalan style. As we have before, let's strike in the center.} 4...d5 5.Bg2 {White develops the bishop... And we'll complete our configuration.} 5...c6 6.O-O {When choosing to play setups like these, White very often quickly activates the kingside and castles. It's time for us to catch up in development. The d6-square is most active for our bishop.} 6...Bd6 7.b3 {7. b3 is the main line at top level and the second most popular move at amateur level. The idea is very often playing Ba3, or Bb2-Qc1-Ba3, in order to trade the dark-squared bishops. Anticipating that, we'll play ...Qe7. Rather than just mindlessly castling, we see White's plan and play against it!} 7...Qe7 8.Bb2 {Realizing they can't go Ba3, White will usually activate the bishop to b2 in this position, having played b3. Their idea, as mentioned, is to play Qc1 and add more support to the a3-square. We've completed kingside development, so let's castle out of the way.} 8...O-O 9.Ne5 {Going for Ne5 here might also happen. Before we challenge the knight, let's make sure we develop our light-squared bishop to b7.} 9...b6 10.cxd5 {White could take on d5.} 10...cxd5 {In this case, we can actually recapture with our c-pawn. This is one of those few rare cases in which we can do this. If White plays Nc3, we can go ...Ba6 to develop the bishop to a great diagonal, but also to cover the b5-square. We'll also put a rook on c8 and start making use of that open c-file.} *

