[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 1: White Plays The Catalan / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/5636b8bd-fbe9-4c16-8ecf-0d0478211458/lines"]

1.d4 {In this chapter, we'll take a look at how to play our already known Stonewall Dutch if White decides to go for various Nf3 setups. Let's dive right in!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White takes advantage of being first to move and places two pawns in the center... ...And we'll build behind the f-pawn to activate our first piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {Nf3 is the third most popular move, being played 17% of the time, but the positions often transpose into the 2 Knights setups. However, if White knows where they're going with this, it's crucial for you to know how to play against the Nf3 setups! We create a pawn chain and open up the dark-squared bishop. We'll have a pawn coming to d5 next!} 3...e6 4.g3 {In this study, we'll cover 4.g3 by White, being the most popular move played 62% of the time. White wants to fianchetto in Catalan style. As we have before, let's strike in the center.} 4...d5 5.Bg2 {White develops the bishop... And we'll complete our configuration.} 5...c6 6.O-O {When choosing to play setups like these, White very often quickly activates the kingside and castles. It's time for us to catch up in development. The d6-square is most active for our bishop.} 6...Bd6 7.Bg5 {If White goes for Bg5, pinning the knight, don't be scared. You've already come across positions like this one.} 7...Nbd7 {We'll simply support the knight, with plans of ...h6 and ...O-O. The good thing about ...h6 is that White can't play Bh4 because of ...g5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bg5 or 4.Bf4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/a966593a-a8b9-41ab-8753-a7695b67e099/lines"]

1.d4 {In Study 2, we'll quickly take a look at Bg5 or Bf4 on move 4 by White. It will be a very short study, as both the moves have already been seen in previous chapters and the positions that arise are very similar. Again, there are transpositions very often.} 1...f5 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll develop the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White will go for the Nf3 setup with 3.Nf3... And we'll prepare ...d5.} 3...e6 4.Bg5 {Placing the bishop to g5 pins our knight on f6 immediately, played only 1% of the time. But it's something we've already seen before. We'll still play ...d5, grabbing space in the center. If White captures on d5, we'll gladly recapture with the e-pawn.} 4...d5 5.e3 {Opening up the light-squared bishop. Let's complete the Stonewall setup of pawns.} 5...c6 6.Bd3 {White will probably develop to d3... And I recommend unpinning the knight with ...Be7 here. Although it looks a little bit passive, it's a sound defensive move. The idea is to free the knight to jump to e4 on the next move.} 6...Be7 7.Qc2 {White's idea might be Qc2, trying to possibly capture in the center and weaken our f5-pawn.} 7...Ne4 {But we're just in time to block their attack! You should be familiar with this position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bg5 or 4.Bf4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/a966593a-a8b9-41ab-8753-a7695b67e099/lines"]

1.d4 {In Study 2, we'll quickly take a look at Bg5 or Bf4 on move 4 by White. It will be a very short study, as both the moves have already been seen in previous chapters and the positions that arise are very similar. Again, there are transpositions very often.} 1...f5 2.c4 {Two pawns in the center. We'll develop the knight to f6.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White will go for the Nf3 setup with 3.Nf3... And we'll prepare ...d5.} 3...e6 4.Bf4 {Going for Bf4 instead of Bg5 simply cannot avoid a transposition into something we've already covered. It's played only 2% of the time. Let's go for our usual ...d5 idea.} 4...d5 5.e3 {White will probably play e3, as in the main line...} 5...c6 {And after ...c6, we've had the exact same position with the knight on c3 already. The knight being on f3 shouldn't change anything, and you know how to fight against Ne5. White will play Nc3 and transpose very often anyway.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 or 4.d5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/2c58e045-d763-4a2c-aec2-ddb36671ce4d/lines"]

1.d4 {It's time to take a look at some of White's "strange" approaches, such as 4.e3 or 4.d5. These moves don't occur very often but can certainly catch us by surprise if we are not theoretically prepared!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White responds with c4, grabbing more central space. Let's activate the knight and control the e4-square with another piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White chooses to play Nf3. We'll prepare the Stonewall with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.e3 {Pushing e3 on move 4 is played only 5% of the time. The obvious downside is that the bishop on c1 is blocked in! Let's see how much our setup should change depending on what White plays. For now, we'll still go ...d5.} 4...d5 5.cxd5 {A move you might face often is capturing in the center immediately. We have an easy decision which pawn to recapture with.} 5...exd5 6.Bd3 {The rest of the moves in this line are pretty standard, and go to show that delaying Nc3 wouldn't help White whatsoever. Here, they attack the f5-pawn. The usual idea is to play Qc2 and add another attacker to it. Let's reinforce the center.} 6...c6 7.O-O {White has developed the kingside, so they castle. We don't have to do anything about the f5-pawn until White attacks it.} 7...Bd6 8.Qc2 {When they play Qc2... We'll go for the same idea as in the previous chapters, blocking the attack.} 8...Ne4 9.Nbd2 {White might challenge our knight, but it is sufficiently defended. Let's just keep developing, with the idea of ...Ndf6. We are also preventing Ne5 for the moment.} 9...Nd7 10.b3 {Again, the most common developing path for the dark-squared bishop is b3 and Bb2/Ba3 when White plays e3. Before castling, let's add support to the a3-square while developing the queen, just in case.} 10...Qe7 11.Bb2 {White activates the bishop.} 11...O-O {And we'll castle. As we've shown, White really should stick to the traditional Nc3 setup. They've achieved nothing developing this way.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 or 4.d5 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/2c58e045-d763-4a2c-aec2-ddb36671ce4d/lines"]

1.d4 {It's time to take a look at some of White's "strange" approaches, such as 4.e3 or 4.d5. These moves don't occur very often but can certainly catch us by surprise if we are not theoretically prepared!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White responds with c4, grabbing more central space. Let's activate the knight and control the e4-square with another piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White chooses to play Nf3. We'll prepare the Stonewall with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.d5 {Pushing d5 right away is played only 1% of the time, but it could definitely become dangerous if we don't know what to do. When White committs early, it's often smart to develop and castle as quickly as possible. Here, we activate the bishop with tempo, giving a check.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Bd2 {The most confrontational approach is to block with the bishop and initiate the tension. We'll take, as retreating would be too passive.} 5...Bxd2+ 6.Qxd2 {If White recaptures with the queen, they preserve the pressure on the d-file. We'll castle out of the way. We don't mind a queen trade if White captures on e6 now, as we've already castled and can recapture with the rook.} 6...O-O 7.Nc3 {White might play Nc3 here to keep developing. Let's go for ...d6, with the idea of ...e5. If White doesn't want to capture, we'll play our own way!} 7...d6 8.dxe6 {They will probably realize our plan and capture now. Now it's even less of a problem, though... As it simply activates our bishop with an attack on the c4-pawn. We'll develop with ...Nc6 next.} 8...Bxe6 9.e3 {White defends by opening up the light-squared bishop...} 9...Nc6 {...And we'll keep developing as well. We've successfully fought against White's aggression on d5!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 or 4.d5 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/2c58e045-d763-4a2c-aec2-ddb36671ce4d/lines"]

1.d4 {It's time to take a look at some of White's "strange" approaches, such as 4.e3 or 4.d5. These moves don't occur very often but can certainly catch us by surprise if we are not theoretically prepared!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White responds with c4, grabbing more central space. Let's activate the knight and control the e4-square with another piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White chooses to play Nf3. We'll prepare the Stonewall with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.e3 {Pushing e3 on move 4 is played only 5% of the time. The obvious downside is that the bishop on c1 is blocked in! Let's see how much our setup should change depending on what White plays. For now, we'll still go ...d5.} 4...d5 5.Bd3 {Another approach instead of c5 is developing the bishop to d3. This is nothing special and something we've already seen. We'll continue with our usual ...c6.} 5...c6 6.O-O {If White castles... We'll develop our bishop to the most active square.} 6...Bd6 7.b3 {Having blocked in their dark-squared bishop, White will most likely develop via b3 and Ba3 to offer a trade of bishops. Once we castle...} 7...O-O 8.Ba3 {They'll do just that. I recommend capturing here and forcing White to activate the knight to an awkward square.} 8...Bxa3 9.Nxa3 {It has to come to a3. Which allows us to activate the queen with tempo. Clumsy play by White.} 9...Qe7 10.Nc2 {Once they retreat...} 10...Nbd7 {We'll activate the queenside knight. Our ideas for developing the c8-bishop are ...b6 and ...Ba6 to target c4. On the kingside and in the center, we'll restrict White even more with ...Ne4 and then often times push ...g5 to start an attack! We can follow it up by getting the rook into the game via ...Rf6 and ...Rh6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 or 4.d5 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/2c58e045-d763-4a2c-aec2-ddb36671ce4d/lines"]

1.d4 {It's time to take a look at some of White's "strange" approaches, such as 4.e3 or 4.d5. These moves don't occur very often but can certainly catch us by surprise if we are not theoretically prepared!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White responds with c4, grabbing more central space. Let's activate the knight and control the e4-square with another piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White chooses to play Nf3. We'll prepare the Stonewall with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.d5 {Pushing d5 right away is played only 1% of the time, but it could definitely become dangerous if we don't know what to do. When White committs early, it's often smart to develop and castle as quickly as possible. Here, we activate the bishop with tempo, giving a check.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Nbd2 {Avoiding doubling the c-pawns, White could block with the knight on d2. Let's castle out of the way. We don't need to capture on d2 in this line and help White develop.} 5...O-O 6.a3 {Once White attacks our bishop... We'll retreat to e7, as retreating to d6 would block our d6-pawn.} 6...Be7 7.e3 {If White doesn't capture on e6 and opens up the light-squared bishop instead... We'll put more pressure on the d5-pawn! Now White has to make a decision.} 7...c6 8.dxc6 {Capturing on c6 splits our central pawns. We'll recapture with the d-pawn in both instances here, against dxc6 and dxe6 too. Recapturing with the d-pawn opens up our queen and controls the d3-square so that White's bishop can't develop there.} 8...dxc6 9.Be2 {So it has to develop to a more passive square like e2.} 9...c5 {Now we'll take the opportunity and play ...c5. Remembering this move is useful for development, as we want to activate the knight to c6 and not block our queen on d8. To expand in the center, we'll try to play ...e5, as nothing is stopping us!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 or 4.d5 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/2c58e045-d763-4a2c-aec2-ddb36671ce4d/lines"]

1.d4 {It's time to take a look at some of White's "strange" approaches, such as 4.e3 or 4.d5. These moves don't occur very often but can certainly catch us by surprise if we are not theoretically prepared!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White responds with c4, grabbing more central space. Let's activate the knight and control the e4-square with another piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White chooses to play Nf3. We'll prepare the Stonewall with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.d5 {Pushing d5 right away is played only 1% of the time, but it could definitely become dangerous if we don't know what to do. When White committs early, it's often smart to develop and castle as quickly as possible. Here, we activate the bishop with tempo, giving a check.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Bd2 {The most confrontational approach is to block with the bishop and initiate the tension. We'll take, as retreating would be too passive.} 5...Bxd2+ 6.Nbxd2 {Recapturing with the knight develops it at the same time. However, it blocks the queen on d1 and lets us recapture on e6 without having to worry about trading queens. We'll castle.} 6...O-O 7.dxe6 {If White captures...} 7...dxe6 {There's no problem. Again, we'll look to expand with ...e5 and develop our knight with ...Nc6.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 or 4.d5 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/2c58e045-d763-4a2c-aec2-ddb36671ce4d/lines"]

1.d4 {It's time to take a look at some of White's "strange" approaches, such as 4.e3 or 4.d5. These moves don't occur very often but can certainly catch us by surprise if we are not theoretically prepared!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White responds with c4, grabbing more central space. Let's activate the knight and control the e4-square with another piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White chooses to play Nf3. We'll prepare the Stonewall with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.d5 {Pushing d5 right away is played only 1% of the time, but it could definitely become dangerous if we don't know what to do. When White committs early, it's often smart to develop and castle as quickly as possible. Here, we activate the bishop with tempo, giving a check.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Bd2 {The most confrontational approach is to block with the bishop and initiate the tension. We'll take, as retreating would be too passive.} 5...Bxd2+ 6.Nbxd2 {Recapturing with the knight develops it at the same time. However, it blocks the queen on d1 and lets us recapture on e6 without having to worry about trading queens. We'll castle.} 6...O-O 7.e3 {Going for a more passive, but definitely solid approach is e3, opening up the light-squared bishop. Do you remember what to do if White delays capturing on e6?} 7...c6 {We add another pawn and force the trade. This trade will bring our pieces to life!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 or 4.d5 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/2c58e045-d763-4a2c-aec2-ddb36671ce4d/lines"]

1.d4 {It's time to take a look at some of White's "strange" approaches, such as 4.e3 or 4.d5. These moves don't occur very often but can certainly catch us by surprise if we are not theoretically prepared!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White responds with c4, grabbing more central space. Let's activate the knight and control the e4-square with another piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White chooses to play Nf3. We'll prepare the Stonewall with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.d5 {Pushing d5 right away is played only 1% of the time, but it could definitely become dangerous if we don't know what to do. When White committs early, it's often smart to develop and castle as quickly as possible. Here, we activate the bishop with tempo, giving a check.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Nc3 {Another move here is Nc3, blocking with the knight on c3. This might not be the best approach, though, because of our next move. We'll capture on c3 and double White's c-pawns.} 5...Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 {Their structure is definitely weakened. We'll deal with White's central pressure by playing ...d6. This enables our bishop to recapture if White decides to capture on e6.} 6...d6 7.dxe6 {Which they most likely will.} 7...Bxe6 {Now our bishop is activated, with pressure on the c4-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 or 4.d5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/2c58e045-d763-4a2c-aec2-ddb36671ce4d/lines"]

1.d4 {It's time to take a look at some of White's "strange" approaches, such as 4.e3 or 4.d5. These moves don't occur very often but can certainly catch us by surprise if we are not theoretically prepared!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White responds with c4, grabbing more central space. Let's activate the knight and control the e4-square with another piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White chooses to play Nf3. We'll prepare the Stonewall with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.e3 {Pushing e3 on move 4 is played only 5% of the time. The obvious downside is that the bishop on c1 is blocked in! Let's see how much our setup should change depending on what White plays. For now, we'll still go ...d5.} 4...d5 5.c5 {Going for c5 is very ambitious, trying to grab space and close down the center. Let's punish White for this way of playing. We'll challenge their structure immediately.} 5...b6 6.b4 {If White defends with b4, we have more to come at them with. Let's attack the b4-pawn as well! White can't play a3, because of the a-file opening, and can't play c3 because the c-pawn is on c5...} 6...a5 7.bxa5 {So they pretty much have to capture and loosen the defence of c5. This works immensely in our favor! We'll capture on c5 and leave the a5-pawn weak.} 7...bxc5 8.dxc5 {Once White recaptures...} 8...Bxc5 {We'll activate our bishop as well. As you can see, White's queenside pawn structure is completely ruined! The a5-pawn is just waiting to get captured.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 or 4.d5 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/2c58e045-d763-4a2c-aec2-ddb36671ce4d/lines"]

1.d4 {It's time to take a look at some of White's "strange" approaches, such as 4.e3 or 4.d5. These moves don't occur very often but can certainly catch us by surprise if we are not theoretically prepared!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White responds with c4, grabbing more central space. Let's activate the knight and control the e4-square with another piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White chooses to play Nf3. We'll prepare the Stonewall with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.e3 {Pushing e3 on move 4 is played only 5% of the time. The obvious downside is that the bishop on c1 is blocked in! Let's see how much our setup should change depending on what White plays. For now, we'll still go ...d5.} 4...d5 5.c5 {Going for c5 is very ambitious, trying to grab space and close down the center. Let's punish White for this way of playing. We'll challenge their structure immediately.} 5...b6 6.cxb6 {If White captures on b6, they've accomplished nothing by pushing the c-pawn forward.} 6...axb6 {We'll simply recapture with the a-pawn and open up our rook. Next, we'll activate the bishop and castle. Playing this way, White has only helped us develop!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: White ONLY Plays Nf3 / Study 3: White Plays 4.e3 or 4.d5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/9b0204cd-f68e-4889-86db-5daa108a88d4/chapters/08c49625-418f-4a80-b6e3-b0c4732b31b0/studies/2c58e045-d763-4a2c-aec2-ddb36671ce4d/lines"]

1.d4 {It's time to take a look at some of White's "strange" approaches, such as 4.e3 or 4.d5. These moves don't occur very often but can certainly catch us by surprise if we are not theoretically prepared!} 1...f5 2.c4 {White responds with c4, grabbing more central space. Let's activate the knight and control the e4-square with another piece.} 2...Nf6 3.Nf3 {White chooses to play Nf3. We'll prepare the Stonewall with ...e6.} 3...e6 4.e3 {Pushing e3 on move 4 is played only 5% of the time. The obvious downside is that the bishop on c1 is blocked in! Let's see how much our setup should change depending on what White plays. For now, we'll still go ...d5.} 4...d5 5.Be2 {Developing the bishop to e2 is simply a more passive and an inferior approach to Bd3. Our play will stay the same, and White won't have the added pressure with the bishop to d3. We'll complete the Stonewall.} 5...c6 6.O-O {Once White castles...} 6...Bd6 {Let's activate the bishop to d6. Our gameplans stay the same here, and any setup White can now play is worse than the one with the bishop on d3.} *